 "scenes": [
  {
   "id": "intro",
   "steps": [
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Module 3 is about doing more than one thing at once. First inside one thread, by overlapping instructions. Then across threads and cores. It all starts with one question: which instructions depend on which?"
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "write",
       "target": "head"
      },
      {
       "action": "replace",
       "target": "head",
       "text": "Dependences and hazards"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Here are the eight stages."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "show",
       "target": "stages"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "What ILP is, then the three kinds of dependence: data, name, and control."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "stages[0..=3]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "How they become the hazards from Module 1, register renaming by hand, a full analysis, and an exam question."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "stages[4..=7]"
      }
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "ilp",
   "steps": [
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Stage 1: instruction-level parallelism, ILP, is overlap between the instructions of one program. A pipeline is the first way to get it."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "replace",
       "target": "head",
       "text": "Stage 1 of 8: what ILP is"
      },
      {
       "action": "hide",
       "target": "stages"
      },
      {
       "action": "show",
       "target": "ilp"
      },
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "ilp[0]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "The pipeline CPI is the ideal CPI plus every kind of stall. All the techniques in this module attack one of those stall terms."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "mark",
       "target": "ilp[0]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "The trouble: branches are 15 to 20 percent of instructions. So a basic block, the straight-line code between two branches, is only about 5 to 7 instructions long. Not much to overlap."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "reveal",
       "target": "ilp[1]"
      },
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "ilp[1]"
      },
      {
       "action": "reveal",
       "target": "ilp[2]"
      },
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "ilp[2]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "So we must find parallelism across branches, and the richest source is loops, where every iteration does similar, often independent, work."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "reveal",
       "target": "ilp[3]"
      },
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "ilp[3]"
      },
      {
       "action": "write",
       "target": "takeaway"
      },
      {
       "action": "replace",
       "target": "takeaway",
       "text": "ILP = overlap inside one thread · limited by dependences"
      }
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "data",
   "steps": [
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Stage 2: data dependences. Here is a loop that adds a scalar in F2 to every element of an array."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "replace",
       "target": "head",
       "text": "Stage 2 of 8: data dependences"
      },
      {
       "action": "hide",
       "target": "takeaway"
      },
      {
       "action": "hide",
       "target": "ilp"
      },
      {
       "action": "show",
       "target": "loop"
      },
      {
       "action": "mark_lines",
       "target": "loop",
       "from": 1,
       "to": 5
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Instruction j is data dependent on instruction i if i produces a result that j uses, or through a chain of such steps."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "show",
       "target": "dd"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "The load writes F0, and the add reads F0. A data dependence."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "goto_line",
       "target": "loop",
       "line": 2
      },
      {
       "action": "set",
       "target": "dd[0][2]",
       "value": "F0"
      },
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "dd[0]",
       "delay": 0.3
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "The add writes F4, and the store reads F4."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "goto_line",
       "target": "loop",
       "line": 3
      },
      {
       "action": "set",
       "target": "dd[1][2]",
       "value": "F4"
      },
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "dd[1]",
       "delay": 0.3
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "And the decrement writes R1, which the branch reads."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "goto_line",
       "target": "loop",
       "line": 5
      },
      {
       "action": "set",
       "target": "dd[2][2]",
       "value": "R1"
      },
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "dd[2]",
       "delay": 0.3
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Load, add, store is a chain: each must wait for the one before. Those three cannot fully overlap."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "mark",
       "target": "dd[0..=1]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Four facts to keep straight. A dependence is a property of the program. A hazard is a property of the pipeline."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "hide",
       "target": "loop"
      },
      {
       "action": "hide",
       "target": "dd"
      },
      {
       "action": "show",
       "target": "ddx"
      },
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "ddx[0]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "So a dependence may or may not cause a stall. With forwarding, and short latencies, often it does not."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "ddx[1]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "But it always limits ILP: the dependent instruction cannot finish before its producer."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "ddx[2]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "And dependences flow through memory too. Does 100 off R4 name the same address as 20 off R6? Often nobody can tell until the program runs."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "ddx[3]"
      },
      {
       "action": "write",
       "target": "takeaway"
      },
      {
       "action": "replace",
       "target": "takeaway",
       "text": "data dependence: a value flows from producer to consumer"
      }
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "name",
   "steps": [
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Stage 3: name dependences. Two instructions use the same register or memory location, but no value flows between them."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "replace",
       "target": "head",
       "text": "Stage 3 of 8: name dependences"
      },
      {
       "action": "hide",
       "target": "takeaway"
      },
      {
       "action": "hide",
       "target": "ddx"
      },
      {
       "action": "show",
       "target": "nd"
      },
      {
       "action": "mark_lines",
       "target": "nd",
       "from": 1,
       "to": 3
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "I1 reads F8. Later, I2 writes F8. If I2 ran first, I1 would read the wrong value. This is an antidependence."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "goto_line",
       "target": "nd",
       "line": 2
      },
      {
       "action": "show",
       "target": "ndx"
      },
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "ndx[0]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "I1 writes F6, and I3 writes F6 too. If they finished in the wrong order, F6 would end with I1's value. This is an output dependence."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "goto_line",
       "target": "nd",
       "line": 3
      },
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "ndx[1]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "The key point: no value flows. I2 does not need anything from I1. They just happen to share a name."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "mark",
       "target": "ndx[0..=1]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "So if we give I2 and I3 different registers, the conflicts vanish. That is register renaming, done by the compiler or by the hardware."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "write",
       "target": "takeaway"
      },
      {
       "action": "replace",
       "target": "takeaway",
       "text": "name dependence: shared name, no value flow · remove by renaming"
      }
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "control",
   "steps": [
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Stage 4: control dependences. Every instruction, except those in the very first block, depends on some branch deciding whether it runs."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "replace",
       "target": "head",
       "text": "Stage 4 of 8: control dependences"
      },
      {
       "action": "hide",
       "target": "takeaway"
      },
      {
       "action": "hide",
       "target": "nd"
      },
      {
       "action": "hide",
       "target": "ndx"
      },
      {
       "action": "show",
       "target": "cd"
      },
      {
       "action": "mark_lines",
       "target": "cd",
       "from": 1,
       "to": 4
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Here the load runs only if R2 is not zero. It is control dependent on the branch."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "goto_line",
       "target": "cd",
       "line": 3
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Rule one: an instruction controlled by a branch cannot simply be moved above it. Then it would run even when the branch says no."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "hide",
       "target": "cd"
      },
      {
       "action": "show",
       "target": "cdx"
      },
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "cdx[0]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Rule two: an instruction not controlled by the branch cannot be moved below it, into its shadow. Then it might be skipped."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "cdx[1]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "What really matters are two properties. Exception behaviour: if we hoisted this load above the branch, and R2 were 0, it could fault, and that fault should never happen."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "cdx[2]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "And data flow: every instruction must read the value from the writer it would have seen in order. Hardware speculation, later in this module, breaks these rules safely."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "cdx[3]"
      },
      {
       "action": "write",
       "target": "takeaway"
      },
      {
       "action": "replace",
       "target": "takeaway",
       "text": "control dependence: keep exceptions and data flow correct"
      }
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "hazards",
   "steps": [
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "Stage 5: how dependences become hazards. Take an instruction i, followed by a later instruction j."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "replace",
       "target": "head",
       "text": "Stage 5 of 8: RAW, WAR and WAW"
      },
      {
       "action": "hide",
       "target": "takeaway"
      },
      {
       "action": "hide",
       "target": "cdx"
      },
      {
       "action": "show",
       "target": "hz"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "A true dependence risks a RAW hazard, read after write: j tries to read before i has written. In our 5-stage pipeline, forwarding and stalls handle it, as in Module 1."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "hz[0]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "An antidependence risks a WAR hazard, write after read: j writes before i has read. Impossible in the 5-stage pipeline, because every instruction reads its registers early, in decode."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "hz[1]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "An output dependence risks a WAW hazard: j writes before i, leaving the wrong final value. Also impossible in order, since every write happens in the same stage, write back."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "hz[2]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "And read after read is never a hazard: reading does not change anything."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "hz[3]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "So WAR and WAW only appear once instructions can finish out of order: long floating-point units, or dynamic scheduling. That is exactly where this module is going."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "mark",
       "target": "hz[1..=2]"
      },
      {
       "action": "write",
       "target": "takeaway"
      },
      {
       "action": "replace",
       "target": "takeaway",
       "text": "true → RAW · anti → WAR · output → WAW"
      }
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
